Suppose instead there are  $2^n$  lightbulbs. Each configuration of light bulbs can be represented by a vector in  $V_n:=\mathbb{F}_2^{\oplus2^n}$ , where  $1$  represents ``on" and  $0$  represents ``off". Let  $T_n$  be the linear map that represents applying the procedure (we prove this is a linear map later). Note that applying the procedure for  $i=0,\,1,\,\ldots,\,n-2$  to a vector  $v\in V_n$  is equivalent to applying the procedure to each the first half of  $v$  and the second half of  $v$  (these both lie in  $V_{n-1}$ ). Hence the corresponding matrix is
\[
\begin{pmatrix}
T_{n-1} & 0
0 & T_{n-1}
\end{pmatrix}.
\]
Note that applying the procedure for  $i=n-1$  to a vector  $v$  is equivalent to adding the first half of  $v$  to the second half. This can be represented by the matrix
\[
\begin{pmatrix}
I_{2^{n-1}} & 0
I_{2^{n-1}} & I_{2^{n-1}}
\end{pmatrix},
\]
where  $I_k$  is the  $k\times k$  identity matrix. Multiplying these two matrices, we get
\[
T_n=
\begin{pmatrix}
I_{2^{n-1}} & 0
I_{2^{n-1}} & I_{2^{n-1}}
\end{pmatrix}
\begin{pmatrix}
T_{n-1} & 0
0 & T_{n-1}
\end{pmatrix}
=
\begin{pmatrix}
T_{n-1} & 0
T_{n-1} & T_{n-1}
\end{pmatrix}.
\]
Note that  $T_0=I_1$ .

If  $T_n^k v=v$  for  $v\in V_n$ , we have  $v\in\mathrm{ker}(T_n^k+I_{2^n})$ .**Claim.**  $T_n^2=I_{2^n}$  for all  $n$ .
*Proof.* Induction on  $n$  with the base case trivial.

For the induction step,
\[
T_n^2=
\begin{pmatrix}
T_{n-1}^2 & 0
0 & T_{n-1}^2
\end{pmatrix}
=
\begin{pmatrix}
I_{2^{n-1}} & 0
0 & I_{2^{n-1}}
\end{pmatrix}
=I_{2^n}.~\square
\]

The answer is thus  $|\mathrm{ker}(T_n+I_{2^n})|+2(2^{2^n}-|\mathrm{ker}(T_n+I_{2^n})|)=2^{2^n+1}-|\mathrm{ker}(T_n+I_{2^n})|$ . It suffices to find  $\dim\mathrm{im}(T_n+I_{2^n})$ , where  $\mathrm{im}~M$  is the image of  $M$ .**Claim.**  $\dim\mathrm{im}(T_n+I_{2^n})=2^{n-1}$  for all  $n\geq 1$ .
*Proof.* For  $v\in V_n$ , let  $a\in V_{n-1}$  be the first half of  $v$  and let  $b\in V_{n-1}$  be the second half, so
\[
v=
\begin{pmatrix}
a
b
\end{pmatrix}.
\]
Note that  $(T_{n-1}+I_{2^{n-1}})^2=T_{n-1}^2+I_{2^{n-1}}=0$ . Hence
\begin{align*}
(T_n+I_{2^n})v&=
\begin{pmatrix}
T_{n-1}+I_{2^{n-1}} & 0
T_{n-1} & T_{n-1}+I_{2^{n-1}}
\end{pmatrix}
\begin{pmatrix}
a
b
\end{pmatrix}
&=
\begin{pmatrix}
(T_{n-1}+I_{2^{n-1}})a
T_{n-1}a+(T_{n-1}+I_{2^{n-1}})b
\end{pmatrix}
&=
\begin{pmatrix}
(T_{n-1}+I_{2^{n-1}})(a+(T_{n-1}+I_{2^{n-1}})b)
T_{n-1}(a+(T_{n-1}+I_{2^{n-1}})b)
\end{pmatrix}
\end{align*}
So  $\dim\mathrm{im}(T_n+I_{2^n})=\dim V_{n-1}=2^{n-1}$ .  $\square$ Thus  $\dim\mathrm{ker}(T_n+I_{2^n})=\dim V_n-\dim\mathrm{im}(T_n+I_{2^n})=2^{n-1}$  so the answer is  $\boxed{2^{2^n+1}-2^{2^{n-1}}}$ . For  $n=2025$ , this is  $\boxed{2^{2^{2025}+1}-2^{2^{2024}}}$ .  $\square$

Consider an initial sequence  $(a_0, a_1, a_2, \dots, a_{2^N - 1})$ , which will always be taken modulo  $2$ , where  $N = 2025$ , where  $a_i = 1$  if light  $i + 1$  is on, and  $0$  otherwise. Then, taking each  $a_i$  modulo  $2$ , the process may be rephrased as follows: For  $i$  from  $0$  to  $2024$     \begin{itemize}
        \item While not all lightbulbs are marked, choose the lightbulb with the smallest number  $n$ , which is unmarked, and add the  $n$ -th element of the list (indexed from  $0$ ) to the  $(n + 2^i)$ -th element of the list.
        \item Unmark all lightbulbs.
    \end{itemize}
\begin{claim}
We now explicitly describe the  $n$ -th element of the list; let  $n = 2^{d_m} + 2^{d_{m - 1}} + \dots + 2^{d_0}$ , with  $d_m > d_{m - 1} > \dots > d_0$ , and define  $\mathcal{P}$  to be the powerset of  $\{d_0, d_1, \dots, d_m\}$ . Then the  $n$ -th element after one day is given by,
\begin{align*}
    f(n) = \sum_{p \in \mathcal{P}} a_{\left({\sum_{d \in p}} 2^d \right)}.
\end{align*}
where the empty set has sum  $0$ . For example, for  $n = 2^2 + 2^0$ , we would let  $\mathcal{P} = \{\varnothing, \{0\}, \{2\}, \{0, 2\}\}$ , and take the sum,
\begin{align*}
    f(5) = \sum_{p \in \mathcal{P}}  a_{\left({\sum_{d \in p}} 2^d \right)} = a_{0} + a_{2^0} + a_{2^2} + a_{2^0 + 2^2} = a_0 + a_1 + a_4 + a_5. 
\end{align*}
\end{claim}
\begin{proof}
To see this we prove it through induction on the number of lights. Assume it has been shown for  $2^n$  lights, where  $0 \leq n < k$ , that the claim holds. We then demonstrate that it must hold for  $n = k$  -- note that the base case of  $k = 0$ , for a single light, is trivial. 
\medskip

\noindent We first demonstrate that while iterating through the process for a single day, for  $i < k - 1$  the first  $2^{k - 1}$  lights do not affect the last  $2^{k - 1}$  lights. This is clear from noting that for  $i = m$ , the lights are marked in blocks of length  $2^{m + 1}$ . In order we mark the pairs of lights,
\begin{align*}
    (1, 2^m + 1) \to (2, 2^m + 2) \to \dots \to (2^m, 2 \cdot 2^{m})
\end{align*}
after which the process repeats  $2^{m + 1}$  lights ahead. Clearly a marked out section of lights cannot affect the next section to be marked.  As  $2^{m + 1} \mid 2^{k - 1}$  it follows that the first  $2^{k - 1}$  lights are all marked before we mark any of the last  $2^{k - 1}$  lights. 
\medskip

Using the previous observation after  $i = 0$ ,  $1$ ,  $\dots$ ,  $k - 2$ , the elements in position  $j$ , indexed by  $0$ , is given by,
\begin{itemize}
    \item  $f(j)$  if  $j \leq 2^{k - 1} - 1$     \item  $g(j) = \sum_{p \in \mathcal{P'}} a_{\left(2^{k - 1} + \sum_{d \in p} 2^d \right)}$  if  $j \geq 2^{k - 1}$ , where  $P' = 2^{d_{m - 1}} + \dots + 2^{d_0}$ , under the assumption that  $j = 2^{k - 1} + 2^{d_{m - 1}} + \dots + 2^{d_0}$ , with  $k - 1 > d_{m - 1} > \dots > d_0$ .
\end{itemize}
which was determined by running  $i = 0$ ,  $1$ ,  $\dots$ ,  $k - 2$  for each half of the  $2^k$  lights separately, and using the inductive hypothesis. 
\medskip

\noindent Finally when  $i = k - 1$ , we find that the elements in position  $j$ , for  $j \geq 2^{k - 1}$ , we add the element in position  $(j - 2^{k - 1})$  to the element in  $j$ , due to the marking process. Thus after step  $i = k - 1$  the element in position  $j$  is given by,
\begin{itemize}
    \item  $f(j)$  if  $j \leq 2^{k - 1} - 1$ , as these elements do not change during the round when  $i = k - 1$ .
    \item  $f(j - 2^{k - 1}) + g(j)$  for  $j \geq 2^{k - 1}$ . By definition this sum is simply equal to  $f(j)$ . 
\end{itemize}
Moreover for  $i > k - 1$ , none of the  $2^k$  elements are affected. Thus it follows that for lists of length  $2^k$ , the  $j$ -th element of the list, indexed by  $0$ , is given by  $f(j)$  after one day. 
\end{proof}
\begin{claim}
    Each sequence  $(a_0, \dots, a_{2^N - 1})$  cycles in at most  $2$  days. 
\end{claim}
\begin{proof}
Note that after a single day the sequence  $(a_0, \dots, a_{2^N - 1}) \mapsto (f(a_0), f(a_1), \dots, f(a_{2^N - 1}))$ , with all elements taken modulo  $2$ . We proceed by induction on the number of elements in the list, to show that after another day each element returns to the value it had on the first day. 
\medskip

Consider the image of the element in the  $j$ -th index, namely  $f(a_j)$ , after the second day. If  $j = 2^{d_m} + 2^{d_{m - 1}} + \dots + 2^{d_0}$ , with  $\mathcal{P}$  being the powerset of  $\{d_0, \dots, d_m\}$ , and  $d_m > \dots > d_0$ , then using our previous claim, the element  $f(a_j)$  is replaced by,
\begin{align*}
     \sum_{p \in \mathcal{P}} f \left( a_{\left({\sum_{d \in p}} 2^d \right)} \right).
\end{align*}
It suffices to show that modulo  $2$  this is the same as  $a_j$ . To do this consider a specific subset of  $\{d_0, \dots, d_m\}$ , say  $s = \{b_0, b_1, \dots, b_{t - 1}, d_\ell\}$ , with maximum element  $d_\ell$ . This subset appears in  $2^{m - t}$  elements of  $\mathcal{P}$ . Hence in the sum,
\begin{align*}
     \sum_{p \in \mathcal{P}} f \left( a_{\left({\sum_{d \in p}} 2^d \right)} \right)
\end{align*}
the element  $a_{\left({\sum_{d \in s}} 2^d \right)}$  appears  $2^{m + 1 - t}$  times, namely whenever  $s$  is a subset of the chosen  $p \in \mathcal{P}$ . The repeated elements add to  $0$  modulo  $2$  as long as  $m + 1 - t \neq 0$ . Therefore all terms of the form  $a_i$  vanish, except for the term  $a_j$ , as  $j$  is associated with the set  $(d_0, \dots, d_m)$ , which is the unique subset of size  $m + 1$ , so  $a_j$  appears only once.
\medskip

For example, the element in the  $5$ -th location satisfies  $5 = 2^2 + 2^0$ . Thus it has powerset  $\mathcal{P} = \{\varnothing, \{ 0\}, \{2 \}, \{0, 2 \} \}$ . Following the second day the element  $f(5)$  is replaced by,
\begin{align*}
         \sum_{p \in \mathcal{P}} f \left( a_{\left({\sum_{d \in p}} 2^d \right)} \right) &= f(5) + f(4) + f(1) + f(0)
         &= (a_5 + a_4 + a_1 + a_0) + (a_4 + a_0) + (a_1 + a_0) + (a_0)
         &= a_5 + 2 \cdot a_4 + 2 \cdot a_1 + 4 \cdot a_0 
         &\equiv a_5 \pmod{2}.
\end{align*}
Hence the sequences cycle with period of either  $1$  or  $2$ . 
\end{proof}
Now for a sequence  $A = (a_0, \dots, a_{2^{k} - 1})$ , with  $k \leq 2025$ , denote the sequence that it is sent to after one day by  $A'$ . Note that  $A'' = A$ . We proceed to prove two properties about the  $'$  operator. 
\begin{claim}
    In general for two sequences of length  $2^k$ , 
    \begin{align*}
        (AB)' = A'(A' \oplus B'),
    \end{align*}
    where  $AB$  denotes the concatenation of two sequences  $A$  and  $B$ .
\end{claim}
\begin{proof}
    As demonstrated in Claim 2.1, the sequences  $A$  and  $B$  are independent for the rounds undergone in a single day while  $i < k$ . Therefore between rounds  $i = k - 1$  and  $i = k$ , the sequence  $AB \mapsto A'B'$ . In the round of  $i = k$ , the  $j$ -th element of  $A'$  adds to the  $j$ -th element of  $B'$ , which when working modulo  $2$ , produces exactly the sequence  $A'B' \mapsto A'(A' \oplus B')$ . 
\end{proof}

\begin{claim}
    For two sequences of length  $2^k$ , say  $A$  and  $B$ ,  $(A \oplus B)' = (A' \oplus B')$ , where  $\oplus$  denotes the exclusive or operator. 
\end{claim}
\begin{proof}
    Note that  $AB = (AB)''$  from our Claim 2.2. However we may expand  $(AB)''$  as,
    \begin{align*}
        AB = (AB)'' = (A'(A' \oplus B'))' = A(A \oplus (A' \oplus B')'),
    \end{align*}
    which implies  $B = A \oplus (A' \oplus B')'$ , to ensure the final  $2^k$  digits match. Using the properties of  $\oplus$  this implies  $A \oplus B = (A' \oplus B')'$ , which in turn leads to the claim  $(A \oplus B)' = (A' \oplus B')$ . 
\end{proof}
Finally we compute the number of one cycles for  $2^{2025}$  lightbulbs. 
\begin{claim}
    On a sequence of length  $2^k$ , there are  $2^{2^{k - 1}}$  elements with one cycles. 
\end{claim}
\begin{proof}
    Consider attempting to build a one cycle of length  $2^k$  from two sequences of length  $2^{k - 1}$ , say  $A$  and  $B$ . In our notation this implies that,
    \begin{align*}
        AB = (AB)' = A'(A' \oplus B')
    \end{align*}
    which implies  $A = A'$  and  $B = A' \oplus B'$ . For  $A = A'$ , we require  $A$  to be an element with a one cycle. For  $B = A' \oplus B'$ , we require  $B \oplus B' = A'$ . Using the fact that  $A = A'$ , we require  $B \oplus B'$  to be an element with a one cycle.  
    \medskip

    This implies that  $B \oplus B' = (B \oplus B')'$ . However as  $'$  distributes over  $\oplus$  from our previous claim,  $B \oplus B' = (B \oplus B')'$  always holds. Hence  $B \oplus B'$  is always an element with a one cycle. 
    \medskip

    This implies we may choose any  $B$  of length  $2^{k - 1}$ , and find a unique working  $A = B \oplus B'$  of length  $2^{k - 1}$  such that  $AB$  is a one cycle of length  $2^k$ . There are  $2^{2^{k - 1}}$  choices for  $B$ , and hence  $2^{2^{k - 1}}$  sequences, of the  $2^{2^{k}}$  possible sequences, of length  $2^k$ , cycle in  $1$  day, with the remaining cycling in  $2$  days. 
\end{proof}
As a result,
\begin{itemize}
    \item  $2^{2^{2024}}$  sequences of lights cycle in  $1$  day.
    \item  $2^{2^{2025}} - 2^{2^{2024}}$  sequences of lights cycle in  $2$  days.
\end{itemize}
Hence the answer is  $2 \cdot 2^{2^{2025}} - 2^{2^{2024}}$ .

