synthetic bash->complex bash->length bash speed run $\textbf{Problem 6:}$  Let ABCDEF be an equiangular hexagon with AB = CD = EF and BC = DE =
FA. Let G be a point on segment AB. Let  $\omega_1$  and  $\omega_2$  be the circumcircles of  $\triangle GAF$ and  $\triangle GBC$  respectively.  $\omega_1$  and  $\omega_2$  meet again at H= G. Let  $\omega_3$  be the circumcircle of  $\triangle DEH$ .  $\omega_3$  meets  $\omega_1$  and  $\omega_2$  again at  $I\ne H$  and  $J\ne H$  respectively. If G,I,J collinear, prove that the areas of  $\triangle FAB$  and  $\triangle FCH$  are equal.

\begin{center}
\centering
\includegraphics[width=1\linewidth]{OMMC2025_P6.jpg}
\end{center}

Diagram clarifications: Let  $O_1,O_2,O_3$  be the centers of  $\omega_1,\omega_2,\omega_3$ , respectively. Since the hexagon is equiangular, extend  $\overline{AF}$ ,  $\overline{BC}$ , and  $\overline{DE}$  to intersect at the vertices of equilateral  $\triangle PQR$  as shown. Point  $I$  does not necessarily lie on  $\overline{EF}$ . Point  $H$  does not necessarily lie on  $(CDQ)$ . $\textbf{Solution 6:}$  Denote side lengths  $s=AB=CD=EF$  and  $t=BC=DE=FA$ . We notice the symmetry of  $\omega_1,\omega_2,\omega_3$ , which pass through sides of length  $t$  and intersect at common point  $H$  along with pairwise  $G,I,J$ . The entire hexagon is cyclic with circumcircle  $\omega$ , and its sides' perpendicular bisectors coincide at the center of  $(PQR)$ . We apply the radical axes theorem to  $\omega$  and each pair of  $\omega_1,\omega_2,\omega_3$  to deduce that the concurrency of lines and the collinearity of  $P\in\overleftrightarrow{GH}$ ,  $Q\in\overleftrightarrow{JH}$ ,  $R\in\overleftrightarrow{IH}$ .

We use directed angles modulo  $180^{\circ}$ . The collinearity condition of  $G,I,J$  is equivalent to  $$ \measuredangle HGI=\measuredangle HGJ\iff\frac12\measuredangle HO_1I=\frac12\measuredangle HO_2J\iff\measuredangle HO_1O_3=\measuredangle HO_2O_3, $$  where we used that  $O_1O_3,O_2O_3$  are the perpendicular bisectors of  $\overline{HI},\overline{HJ}$ , respectively. By using that again, we continue angle chasing  $$ \measuredangle O_1O_3O_2=\measuredangle IHJ=\measuredangle RHQ. $$  The collinearity condition is equivalent to  $H\in(O_1O_2O_3)$  or  $$ \measuredangle O_1O_3O_2=\measuredangle O_1HO_2\iff\measuredangle RHQ=\measuredangle O_2GO_1=\measuredangle AGO_1+\measuredangle O_2GB=180^{\circ}-\measuredangle GFA-\measuredangle BCG. $$  The right-hand-side simplifies to  $$ \measuredangle RHQ=\measuredangle AFG+\measuredangle GCB=360^{\circ}-\left(-60^{\circ}\right)-\measuredangle FGC=\measuredangle CGF+60^{\circ}. $$  We turn to complex bashing to continue on. Denote  $P$  as the origin and the perpendicular bisector of  $\overline{AB}$  as the real axis. For some  $\omega=e^{\pi i/6}$ , we write  $$ a=s\bar\omega,b=s\omega,c=(s+t)\omega,d=(s+t)\omega+s\bar\omega,e=(s+t)\bar\omega+s\omega,f=(s+t)\bar\omega. $$  We know that  $|a||f|=|g||h|$  by the power of the point, and both  $a\bar f$  and  $g\bar h$  are real, so  $$ h=\frac{a\bar f}{\bar g}=\frac{s(s+t)}{\bar g}. $$  Because  $\overline{AB}$  is perpendicular to the real axis,  $g-a$  is imaginary with the equation  $$ g-a=-\overline{(g-a)}\implies g+\bar g=a+b=s\omega+s\bar\omega. $$  Using that  $\omega^2$  has angle  $60^{\circ}$ , we represent the angles with  $$ \frac{\frac{q-h}{r-h}}{\overline{\left(\frac{q-h}{r-h}\right)}}=\frac{\frac{f-g}{c-g}\omega^2}{\overline{\left(\frac{f-g}{c-g}\omega^2\right)}}\implies(c-g)\overline{(f-g)}\bar\omega^2(q-h)\overline{(r-h)}\in\mathbb R, $$  \begin{align*}
&(c-g)\overline{(f-g)}=(c-g)(c-\bar g)&&(q-h)\overline{(r-h)}=(q-h)(q-\bar h)
=&c^2-cg-c\bar g+g\bar g&=&q^2-qh-q\bar h+h\bar h
=&(s+t)^2\omega^2-s(s+t)\omega(\omega+\bar\omega)+g\bar g&=&(2s+t)^2\omega^2-\frac{s^2(s+t)(2s+t)\omega(\omega+\bar\omega)}{g\bar g}+\frac{s^2(s+t)^2}{g\bar g}
=&t(s+t)\omega^2-s(s+t)+g\bar g,&=&\frac{(2s+t)^2\omega^2g\bar g-s^2(s+t)(2s+t)\omega(\omega+\bar\omega)+s^2(s+t)^2}{g\bar g}
&&=&\frac{(2s+t)^2\omega^2g\bar g-s^2(s+t)(2s+t)\omega^2-s^3(s+t)}{g\bar g}.
\end{align*}
When taking the product and dividing by  $\omega^2$ , we disregard  $g\bar g\in\mathbb R$  in the denominator and set  $g\bar g=(s+t)x$ :  $$ \left(t(s+t)\omega^2-s(s+t)+g\bar g\right)\left((2s+t)^2\omega^2g\bar g-s^2(s+t)(2s+t)\omega^2-s^3(s+t)\right)\bar\omega^2\in\mathbb R, $$    $$ \left(t\omega-s\bar\omega+x\bar\omega\right)\left((2s+t)^2\omega x-s^2(2s+t)\omega-s^3\bar\omega\right)\in\mathbb R. $$  From this, we expand out  $$ \omega^2\left((2s+t)^2tx-s^2t(2s+t)\right)+\omega^0(\text{something})+\omega^{-2}\left(s^4-s^3x\right). $$  The something is a real number in terms of  $s,t,x$ . We set the imaginary part to  $0$  to solve  $$ 0=\frac12\left((2s+t)^2tx-s^2t(2s+t)\right)-\frac12\left(s^4-s^3x\right), $$    $$ \left(s^3+4s^2t+4st^2+t^3\right)x=s^4+2s^3t+s^2t^2\implies g\bar g=\frac{s^2(s+t)^2}{s^2+3st+t^2}. $$  We angle chase with cyclic quadrilaterals  $$ \measuredangle CHF=\measuredangle GHF+\measuredangle CHG=\measuredangle GAF+\measuredangle CBG=-240^{\circ}. $$  Since  $\angle BAF=\angle CHF$  without directed angles, we then calculate with similar triangles and law of sines  $$ \frac{[FAB]}{[FCH]}=\frac{AB\cdot AF}{HC\cdot HF}=\frac{st}{\frac{BG\cdot CP}{PG}\cdot\frac{AG\cdot FP}{PG}}=\frac{st\cdot GP^2}{AG\cdot BG(s+t)^2}. $$  We apply Stewart's theorem on  $\overline{PG}$  to rewrite  $$ AG\cdot BG\cdot AB+AB\cdot PG^2=AP^2\cdot BG+BP^2\cdot AG=AB^3. $$  At last, we use  $PG^2=g\bar g$  to finish  $$ \frac{[FAB]}{[FCH]}=\frac{st\cdot GP^2}{(s+t)^2\left(AB^2-GP^2\right)}=\frac{stg\bar g}{s^2(s+t)^2-\left(s^2+2st+t^2\right)g\bar g}=1. $$

