Let the ellipse be tangent to  $CD$  at  $X.$  The following property of ellipses trivializes the problem:
-----**Claim (Reflection Property of Ellipses):** The reflection  $F'$  of  $F$  over  $CD$  lies on  $XE.$ *Proof:* Well-known, but see [ for a good explanation.](https://www.cut-the-knot.org/Curriculum/Geometry/ReflectionInEllipse.shtml#explanation)
-----**Back to the main problem:** Since  $EF = 24\sqrt{2} - 6\sqrt{2} = 18\sqrt{2}$  and  $FF' = 2\cdot \tfrac{3\sqrt{2}}{\sqrt{2}} = 6,$  the Law of Cosines on  $\triangle EFF'$  then gives us
\begin{align*}
EX + FX &= EX + F'X = EF' 
&= \sqrt{EF^2 + FF'^2 - 2\cdot EF\cdot FF'\cdot \cos \angle EFF'} 
&= \sqrt{18^2 \cdot 2 + 6^2 - 2\cdot 18\sqrt{2}\cdot 6\cdot \cos 135^\circ} 
&= 6\sqrt{18 + 1 + 6} 
&= \boxed{30}. 
\end{align*}

