From the definition of  $T$  in part (a), we know that  $\angle BTA = \angle CTA = 180^{\circ} - \angle BAC$ , so  $\angle BTD = \angle CTD = \angle BAC$ . Also, since  $DF \parallel CA$ ,  $\angle BFD = \angle BAC$ , so  $\angle BFD = \angle BTD$ , meaning that  $BFTD$  is cyclic. Similarly,  $CETD$  is cyclic. Then, by Power of a Point,   $$ AF \cdot AB = AT \cdot AD = AE \cdot AC, $$  so  $BFEC$  is cyclic.

Now, consider the three circles  $(AETF)$ ,  $(BFEC)$ , and  $(BTC)$ . The radical center of these circles is  $EF \cap BC = R$ . Then, clearly  $RT$  is the radical axis of  $(AETF)$  and  $(BTC)$ . 

We will now show that  $RT$  is tangent to  $(BTC)$ . Let  $B' = RT \cap (BFTD)$  and  $C' = RT \cap (CETD)$  such that  $B', C' \neq T$ . 

Consider the homothety  $\mathcal{H}$  centered at  $R$  taking  $BF$  to  $DE$ . It is easy to see that  $\mathcal{H}$  takes  $(BFTD)$  to  $(CETD)$ . In particular,  $\mathcal{H}$  maps  $D$  to  $C$ ,  $B'$  to  $T$ , and  $T$  to  $C'$ . Then, since angles are preserved under homotheties,  $\angle B'TB = \angle TC'D$ , and  $\angle TC'D = \angle TCD$  since  $DTC'C$  is cyclic. Thus,  $\angle B'TB = \angle TCD \implies \angle RTB = \angle TCB$ , meaning that  $RT$  is tangent to  $(BTC)$ .

Since  $RT$  is the radical axis of  $(AEF)$  and  $(BTC)$  and is tangent to  $(BTC)$ , it is tangent to  $(AEF)$  as well. Thus,  $(AEF)$  and  $(BTC)$  are tangent at  $T$ .  $\blacksquare$ **<span style="color:#f00">Remark:</span>** Point  $T$  is the midpoint of the  $A$ -symmedian chord of  $(ABC)$ , which also appears in 2019 AIME II #11 and 2008 USAMO #2.

