<details><summary>Hidden for Length</summary>Let  $G$  be the intersection of  $BE$  and  $CF$ . It suffices to prove that  $GH$  and  $EF$  are parallel and have the same slope. Without loss of generality, let  $A=(0,1)$ ,  $B=(b,0)$ , and  $C=(c,0)$ . Note that this uniquely determines the shape of the triangle.
Let  $H=(0,k)$ . We know that the line  $BH$  has slope  $-\frac{1}{-\frac{1}{c}}=\frac{c}{1}=c$ . The slope of line  $BH$  is  $\frac{k}{-b}=c$ , so  $k=-bc$ .  $\textbf{Claim}$ :  $D$  is the reflection of  $H$  over  $BC$ .  $\textbf{Proof}$ : Simple angle chasing. This is quite well-known so the proof will not be included.
Then line  $DE$  has equation  $y=cx+bc$  and Point  $E$  also satisfies  $y=-\frac{1}{c}x+1$ Then  $-\frac{1}{c}x+1=cx+bc$ \begin{align*}
    \implies \left(\frac{1}{c}+c\right)x=1-bc
    \implies x=\frac{1-bc}{c+\frac{1}{c}}
    =\frac{c-bc^2}{c^2+1}.
\end{align*}
The  $y$ -coordinate of  $E$  is then  $-\frac{1}{c}\left(\frac{c-bc^2}{c^2+1}\right)+1=-\frac{1-bc}{1+c^2}+1$ Thus  $E=\left(\frac{c-bc^2}{c^2+1},-\frac{1-bc}{1+c^2}+1\right)$ . Similarly,  $F=\left(\frac{b-b^2c}{1+b^2}, -\frac{1-bc}{1+b^2}+1\right)$ .  $\textbf{Claim:}$  The equation of the line through  $(m,n)$  and  $(p,q)$  is   $$ y=\left(\frac{q-n}{p-m}\right)x+\left(n-\frac{q-n}{p-m}\cdot m\right) $$   $\textbf{Proof:} $ The slope part is trivial. Then we can plug in  $x=m$  and  $y=n$  into  $y=\left(\frac{q-n}{p-m}\right)x+b$  and solve for  $b$ . The result follows.
By our Claim, line  $BE$  has equation   $$ y=\left(\frac{-\frac{1-bc}{1+c^2}+1}{\frac{c-bc^2}{1+c^2}-b}\right)x+\left(-\frac{-\frac{1-bc}{1+c^2}+1}{\frac{c-bc^2}{1+c^2}-b}\cdot b\right) $$   and  $CF$  has equation   $$ y=\left(\frac{-\frac{1-bc}{1+b^2}+1}{\frac{b-b^2c}{1+b^2}-c}\right)x+\left(-\frac{-\frac{1-bc}{1+b^2}+1}{\frac{b-b^2c}{1+b^2}-c}\cdot c\right) $$  But   $$ \left(\frac{-\frac{1-bc}{1+c^2}+1}{\frac{c-bc^2}{1+c^2}-b}\right) $$  \begin{align*}
=\frac{-1+bc+1+c^2}{c-bc^2-b-bc^2}
=\frac{bc+c^2}{c-b-2bc^2}
\end{align*} So  $BE$  has equation   $$ y=\left(\frac{bc+c^2}{c-b-bc^2}\right)x+\left(\frac{bc+c^2}{c-b-2bc^2}\cdot (-b)\right) $$   and similarly  $CF$  has equation    $$ y=\left(\frac{bc+b^2}{b-c-b^2c}\right)x+\left(\frac{bc+b^2}{b-c-2b^2c}\cdot (-c)\right). $$  \textbf{Claim}: The intersection of  $y=\alpha x-\alpha b $  and  $y=\beta x-\beta c$  is   $$ \left(\frac{-\alpha\cdot b+\beta \cdot c}{\beta - \alpha },\beta x-\beta c\right)=\left(\frac{-\alpha\cdot b+\beta \cdot c}{\beta - \alpha },\beta(x-c)\right) $$   where the  $x$  in the  $y$ -coordinate is the  $x$ -coordinate. The proof is simple algebraic manipulation.
By our Claim, the  $x$ -coordinate of  $G$  is  $\frac{-\left(\frac{bc+c^2}{b-c-b^2c}\right)\cdot b + \left(\frac{bc+b^2}{b-c-b^2c}\right)\cdot c}{\left(\frac{bc+c^2}{c-b-bc^2}\right)-\left(\frac{bc+b^2}{b-c-b^2c}\right)}$ \begin{align*}
=bc\left(\frac{-\frac{c+b}{c-b-2bc^2}+\frac{c+b}{b-c-2b^2c}}{\frac{b(b+c)}{b-c-2b^2c}-\frac{c(b+c)}{c-b-2bc^2}}\right)
=bc\left(\frac{-\frac{1}{c-b-2bc^2}+\frac{1}{b-c-2b^2c}}{\frac{b}{b-c-2b^2c}-\frac{c}{c-b-2bc^2}}\right)
=bc\left(\frac{\frac{-b+c+2b^2c+c-b-2bc^2}{(c-b-2bc^2)(b-c-2b^2c)}}{\frac{bc-b^2-2b^2c^2-bc+c^2+2b^2c^2}{(c-b-2bc^2)(b-c-2b^2c)}}\right)
=bc\left(\frac{2(-b+c+b^2c-bc^2)}{c^2-b^2}\right)
=bc\left(\frac{2(c-b)(1-bc)}{(c-b)(b+c)}\right)=\frac{2bc(1-bc)}{b+c}
\end{align*}
and the  $y$ -coordinate of  $G$  is 
\begin{align*}
    (x-c)\cdot\frac{b^2+bc}{b-c-2b^2c}
    =\left(\frac{2bc(1-bc)}{b+c}-c\right)\left(\frac{b^2+bc}{b-c-2b^2c}\right)
    =\frac{2bc-2b^2c^2-bc-c^2}{b+c}\cdot\frac{b^2+bc}{b-c-2b^2c}
    =\frac{2bc-2b^2c^2-bc-c^2}{b+c}\cdot\frac{b(b+c)}{b-c-2b^2c}
    =\frac{2b^2c-2b^3c^2-b^2c-bc^2}{b-c-2b^2c}
    =bc\left(\frac{2b-2b^2c-b-c}{b-c-2b^2c}\right)
\end{align*}

Since  $H=(0,-bc)$  and  $G=\left(\frac{2bc(1-bc)}{b+c},bc\left(\frac{2b-2b^2c-b-c}{b-c-2b^2c}\right)\right)$ ,  $GH$  has slope 
\begin{align*}
    \frac{bc\left(\frac{2b-2b^2c-b-c}{b-c-2b^2c}+1\right)}{\frac{2bc(1-bc)}{b+c}}
    =\frac{\left(\frac{2b-2b^2c-b-c}{b-c-2b^2c}+1\right)}{\frac{2(1-bc)}{b+c}}
    =\frac{\left(\frac{2b-4b^2c-2c}{b-c-2b^2c}\right)}{\frac{2(1-bc)}{b+c}}
    =\frac{\left(\frac{b-2b^2c-c}{b-c-2b^2c}\right)}{\frac{1-bc}{b+c}}
    =\frac{\left(\frac{b-2b^2c-c}{b-c-2b^2c}\right)(b+c)}{1-bc}
    =\frac{b+c}{1-bc}.
\end{align*}

Since  $E=\left(\frac{c-bc^2}{c^2+1},-\frac{1-bc}{1+c^2}+1\right)$  and  $F=\left(\frac{b-b^2c}{1+b^2}, -\frac{1-bc}{1+b^2}+1\right)$ , the slope of  $EF$  is 
\begin{align*}
    \frac{(-\frac{1-bc}{1+c^2}+1)-(-\frac{1-bc}{1+b^2}+1)}{\frac{1-bc}{1+c^2}\cdot c-\frac{1-bc}{1+b^2}\cdot b}
    =\frac{(-\frac{1-bc}{1+c^2}+1)-(-\frac{1-bc}{1+b^2}+1)}{\frac{c-bc^2}{1+c^2}-\frac{b-b^2c}{1+b^2}}
    =\frac{-(1+b^2-bc-b^3c)+(1-bc+c^2-bc^3)}{-(c-bc^2+b^2c-b^3c^2)+(b+bc^2-b^2c-b^2c^3)}
    =\frac{(c^2-b^2)(1-bc)}{(c-b)(1-2bc+b^2c^2)}
    =\frac{(c^2-b^2)(1-bc)}{(c-b)(1-bc)^2}
    =\frac{c+b}{1-bc}
\end{align*}
so  $GH$  and  $EF$  are indeed parallel, as desired.</details>

