solution_md:
Suppose Willis's carrier is a quantum superposition of the following  $16$  positions:
[asy]
size(5cm);
real d = 0.1;
int n = 9;
path rect(int a, int b, int x, int y)
{
return (a-1+d,b-1+d)--(x-d,b-1+d)--(x-d,y-d)--(a-1+d,y-d)--cycle;
}
for (int i = 0; i <= n; ++i)
{
draw((i,0)--(i,n));
draw((0,i)--(n,i));
}
for (int j = 0; j < 4; ++j)
{
fill(rect(1,j + 1,5,j + 1),red);
fill(rect(j + 6,1,j + 6,5),red);
fill(rect(5,j + 6,9,j + 6),red);
fill(rect(j + 1,5,j + 1,9),red);
}
[/asy]
Then Phyllis must shoot at least one square in each of the  $16$  positions, so Phyllis must take at least  $16$  shots. On the other hand, these shots certainly work:
[asy]
size(5cm);
for (int i = 0; i <= 9; ++i)
{
draw((i,0)--(i,9));
draw((0,i)--(9,i));
}
real d = 0.1;
for (int i = 1; i <= 9; ++i)
{
if (i != 5) {
draw((i - 1 + d,4 + d)--(i - d,5 - d));
draw((i - 1 + d,5 - d)--(i - d,4 + d));
draw((4 + d,i - 1 + d)--(5 - d,i - d));
draw((4 + d,i - d)--(5 - d,i - 1 + d));
}
}
[/asy]
So the answer is  $\boxed{16}$  as desired.

